### Tone: Rule slide: points. The closest answer scores the most, and being very close earns a bonus. Dry, bureaucratic.
### The final question counts double (a few lines may say so). Never mention dunce caps: they're an easter egg, found by being wildly off, not a rule to point towards.

rules-points-1 = The closer you are, the more points you get. Get very close and there’s a bonus. Get very far and there’s a lesson.
rules-points-2 = Points go by rank: closest first. Land within a hair of the truth for a bonus. I will be grading. Harshly. Fairly. Harshly.
rules-points-3 = Rank decides your points. Precision earns a bonus. Wild guesses earn my full attention.
rules-points-4 = The closest answer wins the most points, the next closest a little less, and so on, down to whoever you are. The last question pays double.
rules-points-5 = Points by rank. Bonus for being close. Extra bonus for being very close. Nothing at all for being confident.
rules-points-6 = Within twice the truth earns a small bonus. Within ten percent of it earns a bullseye. I have not seen many bullseyes.
rules-points-7 = Points go to whoever is closest, then the next closest, and so on. Distance, measured in times off, is everything.
rules-points-8 = Every round, answers are ranked from closest to furthest. Points follow the ranking. The final question counts double. Excuses never count.
rules-points-9 = Get it nearly right for a bonus. Get it wildly wrong for a mention in my report. Both are recorded permanently.
rules-points-10 = Closest answer, most points. Within double, a bonus. Within ten percent, a bigger one. Missing by miles earns nothing but my notes.
